    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Lorenzo takes its name from Lorenzo Dow, an employee of the C.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/lorenzo-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The first train rolled through Lorenzo in 1911, and that same year Viola Ellison taught the town’s first school classes in a storefront.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.tshaonline.org/handbook/entries/lorenzo-tx"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Lorenzo sits on the edge of urban and rural, letting you spot Lubbock’s lights at night while hearing tractors at dawn.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitcrosbycounty.com/history/lorenzo/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After dryland cotton yields stabilized, Lorenzo grew into a steady producer of cotton, grain sorghum, onions, and sunflowers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitcrosbycounty.com/history/lorenzo/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Walk down Lorenzo’s main strip to see the Mural Corridor, a series of large‑scale artworks that celebrate the town’s history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://visitcrosbycounty.com/history/lorenzo/"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Lorenzo is often the first face of Crosby County for travelers coming from Lubbock.",
